{"judgments": [
  {"clause_id": "c051", "issues": [
    {"issue": "This paragraph makes 11.1 govern vacation \"notwithstanding anything contained herein or in University rules, regulations or policies to the contrary,\" which overrides the incorporation of Staff Handbook fringe benefits in provision 3.2. Whether Handbook vacation payout rights survive depends on reading the two together.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.13}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c052", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The University's \"allotted budget\" for football staff \"shall not be less than One Million Nine Hundred Thousand Dollars ($1,900,000) per Contract Year,\" yet the Employee must keep costs \"within the University's allotted budget,\" leaving unclear whether the floor is also the ceiling. The Employee's authority to \"establish the salaries of\" staff is at the same time made \"subject to the prior approval\" of the Director of Athletics and \"University policy.\"", "type": 1, "prob": 0.20}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c053", "issues": [
    {"issue": "Each assistant \"may be paid\" an extra month's salary on the Employee's recommendation and the Director's approval, so it is unclear whether any entitlement is created at all. The provision fixes deadlines for a payment that no one is bound to make.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.13}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c054", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The provision lets the University \"at any time, with just cause, terminate this Contract or suspend the Employee with or without pay\" and thereby be \"relieved of all further obligations,\" but the operative term \"just cause\" is left to the definition in the following paragraph and to its \"normally understood meaning.\" Whether conduct short of the enumerated grounds supports termination is the central question the words leave open.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.45},
    {"issue": "The relationship with provision 14.2 is unstated: if a purported just-cause termination is found unjustified, nothing says whether it converts into a unilateral termination without just cause carrying $750,000 per Contract Year in liquidated damages, or leaves the Employee to ordinary breach damages. The two termination routes are drafted as alternatives with no bridge between them.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.32}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c055", "issues": [
    {"issue": "Ground A requires \"conduct by Employee resulting in his conviction of a violation of a criminal statute,\" yet ground F lets the University terminate for any conduct it \"determin[es]\" is \"seriously prejudicial to the best interests of the University\" or that \"brings the University into disrepute\" or \"reflects ... moral turpitude.\" Whether criminal conduct that produces an arrest or diversion but no conviction is just cause turns on whether the specific ground A limits the general ground F.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.55},
    {"issue": "\"[J]ust cause\" is defined to mean the listed grounds \"in addition to its normally understood meaning in employment contracts,\" importing an undefined external standard alongside the enumeration. The definition therefore does not close, and its outer limit must be construed.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.38}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c056", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The liquidated damages are fixed \"in, and only in, the amount of Seven Hundred Fifty Thousand Dollars ($750,000) per Contract Year, pro rated, for the then-unexpired Term,\" a figure that does not correspond to any compensation component in provisions 3.1, 3.9 or 8.4 and that leaves \"pro rated\" for the thirteen-month first Contract Year unexplained. What the Employee actually recovers on a no-cause termination is not calculable from the words alone.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.32},
    {"issue": "The exclusive-remedy language cannot be squared with provision 24.6, which makes \"[a]ll remedies, rights, undertakings, obligations and agreements ... cumulative\" and preserves every other remedy available at law or in equity. Whether $750,000 per year is the Employee's sole recovery depends on which of the two controls.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.24}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c057", "issues": [
    {"issue": "Liquidated damages are \"paid as if salary, in equal monthly installments in the manner described in provision 3.1,\" without saying whether the fringe benefits and other salary-linked entitlements of provision 3.2 also continue. The deemed \"separation from service\" characterization compounds the uncertainty about the payments' nature.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.16}
  ]},
